  <h1>India’s Malnutrition Challenge — NFHS-5 and Policy Responses</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Malnutrition remains one of India’s gravest human development challenges despite decades of welfare schemes. Indian Express in 2024 analyzed NFHS-5 (2019–21) data and recent government interventions, noting persistent gaps in child stunting, wasting, and anaemia. Tackling malnutrition is central to India’s pursuit of SDG-2: Zero Hunger and is a recurring UPSC theme under health, governance, and social justice.
________________________________________
NFHS-5 Key Findings
•	Stunting: 35.5% of children under 5.
•	Wasting: 19.3% of children under 5.
•	Underweight: 32% of children.
•	Anaemia:
o	57% of women (15–49 years).
o	67% of children (6–59 months).
These figures indicate only modest improvement from NFHS-4 and, in some cases (anaemia), deterioration.
________________________________________
Causes of Malnutrition
1.	Food Insecurity
o	Over-dependence on cereals under PDS; lack of protein and micronutrients.
2.	Maternal Health
o	High levels of anaemia and poor maternal nutrition.
3.	Sanitation & Hygiene
o	Open defecation and unsafe drinking water contributing to diarrheal diseases.
4.	Poverty & Inequality
o	Poor households unable to diversify diets.
5.	Behavioural Factors
o	Inadequate breastfeeding and child feeding practices.
________________________________________
Government Interventions
•	POSHAN Abhiyaan (2018): Convergence approach to reduce stunting, wasting, anaemia.
•	Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS): Anganwadi-based supplementary nutrition.
•	Midday Meal Scheme (PM POSHAN): Hot cooked meals for schoolchildren.
•	Anaemia Mukt Bharat (2018): Targeting iron and folic acid supplementation.
•	Food Fortification: Rice fortification in PDS and midday meals.
________________________________________
Challenges
•	Implementation Gaps: Leakages in PDS and ICDS services.
•	Quality Issues: Poor nutrition quality of Anganwadi and school meals.
•	Monitoring Deficit: Lack of real-time data for course correction.
•	State Variations: Malnutrition worse in states like Bihar, UP, Jharkhand.
•	Urban Malnutrition: Rising among slum children, often overlooked.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-II (Governance & Social Justice): Welfare schemes, child development.
•	GS-III (Health & Economy): Nutrition, poverty, human capital.
•	Essay:
o	“Nutrition is not only about food, but also about equity and dignity.”
o	“Human development is the true measure of a nation’s progress.”
•	PSIR Paper II: Welfare state and social policy in India.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Diet Diversification: Strengthen pulses, eggs, and dairy in PDS and school meals.<br/>2.	Community Involvement: Women’s SHGs in nutrition monitoring and meal preparation.<br/>3.	Behavioural Change: Awareness on breastfeeding and infant feeding practices.<br/>4.	Strengthen Anganwadis: Upgrade infrastructure, training, and digital monitoring.<br/>5.	Targeted Interventions: Focus on high-burden states with region-specific strategies.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s malnutrition crisis undermines its demographic dividend and economic future. While policies like POSHAN Abhiyaan and food fortification are steps forward, the challenge lies in implementation, quality, and inclusivity. For UPSC aspirants, malnutrition is a cross-cutting theme linking poverty, health, gender, governance, and human rights.</p>
